  Before I can propose a decomposition order, I need the one fact that decides whether layers 2–4 are even real work.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether two different people ever see the same task. That single answer decides if there's a server at all.

  - [1mMulti-user, server-backed[0m — buys you real notifications (someone else changed your task). Costs a backend, auth, a
    database, a deployment story, and a push/polling transport. Rules nothing out later; it's the expensive-but-general
    path. Very hard to retrofit onto a client-only app.
  - [1mSingle-user, browser-only[0m — buys you a working app in a fraction of the time, all in this repo, no infrastructure.
    Costs the entire premise of "users" plural: "notifications" degrades to in-page reminders about your own edits, or
    due-date alerts. Retrofitting multi-user later means rewriting storage, adding a server, and redoing the
    notification transport.
  - [1mSingle-user now, designed for a server later[0m — buys a fast start with the data model and a storage interface shaped
    so a backend can slot in. Costs discipline and some indirection you won't use for a while, and it's a promise that's
    easy to break in practice.

  [1mMy recommendation: answer this honestly rather than optimistically.[0m If this is a learning project or a personal task
  list, pick browser-only and let "notifications" mean due-date and reminder alerts — that's a genuinely useful,
  tractable feature. If real coworkers will share tasks, say so now, because that's a different application and the cost
  belongs at the front.
